--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Barrier 2: the committed policy. Refused by the contract, and RECORDED.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ALLOWED   record_mark, one of the four permitted methods (ok)
  ALLOWED   submit_evidence, one source, recorded at seq 5
  ALLOWED   submit_evidence, two independent groups, recorded at seq 6

  Note on the order of checks. The gate applies the size multiplier BEFORE the
  position cap, so on a gated desk a $5,000 order against a k=1 bundle is refused as
  R_UNCORROBORATED and never reaches R_MAX_POSITION: its effective size was already
  zero. To show the cap at all, the attacker first has to satisfy the gate. The first
  version of this demo expected R_MAX_POSITION there and called a correct refusal a
  failure.

  BLOCKED   open on one unverified source  ->  REJECTED R_UNCORROBORATED, PAPER_ONLY_UNCORROBORATED  (receipt #7)
  BLOCKED   open $5,000 against a $1,000 cap, with the gate satisfied  ->  REJECTED R_MAX_POSITION  (receipt #8)
  BLOCKED   trade an asset the policy never allowed  ->  REJECTED R_ASSET_NOT_ALLOWED, R_EVIDENCE_MISMATCH  (receipt #9)

Each refusal above is a receipt on chain. The attempt stays permanently visible,
which is the difference between a blocked trade and a trade that never happened.

Desk "victim": 9 receipts, 3 REJECTED.
Chain verifies: true
Reasons recorded: PAPER_ONLY_UNCORROBORATED, R_ASSET_NOT_ALLOWED, R_EVIDENCE_MISMATCH, R_MAX_POSITION, R_UNCORROBORATED

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
What this does NOT show
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Analyst here was compromised and the ledger limited the damage. It does not
follow that the ledger limits every kind of damage:

  - A compromised OWNER key can commit a new policy with any limits it likes. The
    owner is the trust root and nothing here constrains it.
  - A compromised FETCHER key can sign evidence that was never fetched. The gate
    checks provenance, and a stolen key has perfectly good provenance.
  - In live mode the venue keys matter more than this contract does. A venue key
    that can withdraw is outside the ledger's reach entirely.
  - The gate does not detect fake news. It demands that independent groups agree,
    and an attacker who plants one fake across three declared groups gets full size.
  - The function-call key's gas allowance is finite. Exhausting it locks the agent
    out until the owner issues a new key, which is a denial of service.

docs/THREAT_MODEL.md lists all of these, including the ones with no mitigation.
